From 1aa74989c82873b2fa203a7d1692b365d2b65e6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: whn09 <wanghenan09@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:59:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] vdn: test the parallel VAE decode against the serial
 one on fixed latents

Patch 0005 claims its eight-rank decode is bit-identical to vae.decode(), and the grid was
checking that by rendering the same prompt both ways and running `cmp` on the two mp4s. That
check reported MISMATCH at both canvases, and the check was wrong, not the decode.

**This pipeline does not reproduce run to run.** Measured, for identical configurations run
twice -- same seed, same config, same split, same decode path:

    480p r3, parallel decode both times     17.55 dB PSNR
    768p r0, serial decode both times       16.69 dB PSNR

Not one frame of 345 is bit-identical in either pair. The two renders are the same video:
temporal cross-correlation peaks at shift 0 and falls 5 dB by four frames either side, and
16x-downsampled PSNR climbs only to ~22-29 dB where independent high-frequency noise would
have gained ~33 dB over that reduction. So the runs agree on scene, composition and motion
and disagree on detail everywhere -- ULP divergence in the fp8 GEMMs, amplified over eight
sampler steps. An end-to-end diff therefore compares two denoise trajectories and reports a
mismatch whatever the decoder does.

So the decode is tested where it lives: one process, one latent tensor, decoded both ways
back to back, with nothing upstream in the comparison. Latents come from a seeded generator
because the decoder is a pure function of its input; --latents takes a real render's tensor
for anyone who would rather check in-distribution values.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
